---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import MidContentAd from '../components/shared/MidContentAd.astro';
import EndOfContentAd from '../components/shared/EndOfContentAd.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/vitamin-d-and-tuberculosis`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Vitamin D and Tuberculosis',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  about: [{ '@type': 'MedicalCondition', name: 'Tuberculosis', code: { '@type': 'MedicalCode', codeValue: 'A15', codingSystem: 'ICD-10' } }],
  lastReviewed: '2026-08-14',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Vitamin D and Tuberculosis', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Vitamin D and Tuberculosis: The Sanatorium, Cathelicidin, and the Trials"
  description="Before antibiotics, TB patients were sent to sun-drenched sanatoriums. Vitamin D genuinely helps macrophages kill the TB bacterium — but do supplement trials speed recovery?"
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamin-d-and-tuberculosis", label: "Vitamin D & Tuberculosis" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Vitamin D and Tuberculosis</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Long before anyone had isolated vitamin D, doctors noticed that tuberculosis patients did better
      in the sun. The sanatorium movement of the 19th and early 20th centuries sent people with TB to
      sunny, mountain-air retreats, and "heliotherapy" — deliberate sun exposure — was a mainstream
      treatment. It turns out the biology behind that folk observation is real, even if the modern
      supplement trials are more nuanced.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">The cathelicidin mechanism</h2>
    <MidContentAd />
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      When a macrophage engulfs <em>Mycobacterium tuberculosis</em>, the bacterium's cell-wall
      components trigger the immune cell to activate vitamin D locally, converting 25(OH)D into the
      active hormone right at the site. That active vitamin D switches on the gene for
      <strong>cathelicidin</strong>, an antimicrobial peptide that helps the macrophage kill the
      mycobacterium (Liu 2006). This is one of the most elegant and well-documented immune roles of
      vitamin D — and it means a macrophage starved of 25(OH)D is a less effective TB-killer. It also
      neatly explains the sanatorium-era observation.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Deficiency and risk</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Low vitamin D is common in people with active TB, and some studies link deficiency and certain
      vitamin D receptor gene variants to higher susceptibility. As always, direction is uncertain —
      chronic illness and reduced sun exposure lower vitamin D too — but the mechanistic link makes a
      causal contribution plausible in a way it is not for many other conditions.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What the treatment trials show</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Randomised trials of vitamin D <em>added to standard TB antibiotics</em> have been mixed on the
      headline outcome (time to sputum culture conversion). The most important finding came from an
      individual-patient-data meta-analysis (Jolliffe 2019): overall, vitamin D did not significantly
      speed culture conversion — <strong>but</strong> in the subgroup with multidrug-resistant TB, it
      did accelerate clearance. In other words, standard TB drugs do the heavy lifting; vitamin D
      appears to add measurable value mainly where the drugs are struggling. Adjunctive vitamin D is
      cheap and safe, so it is a reasonable add-on in deficient patients, not a treatment on its own.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The takeaway</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      TB is one of the clearest cases where vitamin D has a real, mechanistic immune role — the
      cathelicidin pathway is textbook immunology, not wishful thinking. But "real mechanism" does not
      equal "cure": antibiotics remain the treatment, and vitamin D is a supportive adjunct whose
      biggest measured benefit is in drug-resistant disease.
    </p>

    <EndOfContentAd />

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> Tuberculosis is a serious, notifiable infection that
      requires a full course of prescribed antibiotics under medical supervision. Vitamin D never
      substitutes for that treatment.
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/vitamin-d-and-tuberculosis" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
